// src/std/core/range.zen
// what every loop word walks.
//
// DESIGN.md types the loop family's first parameter `range: Range`
// and then passes it a fixed array, a Vec and a Map, without ever
// declaring Range. Both readings it leaves open are true at once,
// and one name carries both:
//
//   CONCRETE. `Range(0, 5)` constructs one. start and end are
//   ordinary storage, half-open, so Range(0, 5) is five passes and
//   the off-by-one that makes it six has a corpus test.
//
//   A BOUND. `Range` is an ordinary struct used as a bound, which
//   is all a bound ever is here (DESIGN.md: "a bound is just which
//   struct's shape do I need"). A Vec satisfies it exactly as
//   Circle satisfies Rect - by supplying values for the fields it
//   does not store.
//
// So the family is written `<R: Range<T>, T> = (range: R, ..)` and
// not `(range: Range<T>, ..)`. The two are not the same thing:
// `alloc: Alloc` establishes that an interface-TYPED parameter is a
// fat value carrying function pointers, and a function pointer read
// per element is not a plain C for-loop. A bound monomorphises, so
// `at` is a direct call, the body inlines, and tests/bench/
// bench_loop.zen can gate a fold at allocs_op: 0 and mean it.
//
// Nothing is impl'd here. A trait sits BELOW the types that satisfy
// it, so an impl lives with the type - the two impls this file
// needs are named at the bottom, and neither of them is ours.

Res = std.core.result

// a half-open index interval [start, end), plus what lives at an
// index. `at` is the ONE thing an impl supplies: walking is
// at(start), at(start + 1), .. and a None ends the walk early, so a
// container whose length moved under the loop stops rather than
// reading past itself.
//
// `at` is an optional hook (`::=` with no body): a Range that
// supplies none walks its own index space. That is what makes
// `Range(0, 5)` mean 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 with nothing behind it, its fold
// a bare C for-loop over a usize counter, and `Range(0, v.len)` the
// ordinary way to walk indices rather than elements - which is how
// std.collections already uses it.
Range*<T> = {
    start*: usize,
    end*: usize,

    at* ::= (self: @Self, index: usize) Res<T>
}

// THE TWO IMPLS THIS FILE SPECIFIES AND DOES NOT OWN
//
// A VEC is a Range of its elements, and the impl is one line
// because `get` already answers exactly the question `at` asks -
// including the None past the end. It belongs in
// std.collections.collections_vec, beside the type, and this is the
// whole of it:
//
//     Vec.impl(Range<T>, {
//         start: 0,
//         end: self.len,
//         at ::= (self: @Self, index: usize) Res<T> { self.get(index) }
//     })
//
// start and end are supplied, not stored, so a Vec stays exactly as
// wide as it was and a Vec that grows mid-loop reports its new end
// rather than a stale one.
//
// A FIXED ARRAY satisfies Range intrinsically, and it is the one
// place the compiler supplies what no module could have written:
// `[T, N]` is a type constructor, not a name, so there is no
// `[T, N].impl(..)` to write - the impl target in the grammar is an
// identifier, and there is no module that declares `[T, N]` for the
// impl to live in. Its start is 0, its end is the comptime length,
// and its `at` is the bounds-checked index that already traps.
